diagnosis_text:
The reported fault does not exist. A direct ping from Laptop2 (172.16.10.12)
to Laptop3 (172.16.10.13) completes with 4/4 packets received and zero packet
loss. Inspection of switch1 confirms that both access ports serving the two
laptops — eth3 (Laptop2, cd_sw1_fa3_1) and eth4 (Laptop3, cd_sw1_fa4_1) —
are administratively and operationally UP, carry zero error and drop counters,
and are in the FORWARDING state on the br_vlan10 Linux bridge. STP is disabled
on the bridge so there is no blocking or listening state to blame. Both endpoint
MAC addresses (c6:f3:3a:95:3f:3e for Laptop2 and d6:a0:24:66:51:7f for
Laptop3) are dynamically learned in the br_vlan10 FDB on their respective ports,
confirming bidirectional L2 reachability. No traffic-control filters or
policers are attached to either port. The ticket's premise — that switch1 is
dropping frames or that a VLAN 10 port is err-disabled or blocking — is
incorrect. The switching fabric for VLAN 10 on switch1 is operating exactly as
designed. The user should verify their endpoint configuration (e.g., host
firewall rules, ARP cache, or application-layer issue) as the most likely source
of any perceived connectivity problem.
=== END ===
